For the exact shape of error response bodies, see the Error Handling section.

### Post-migration checklist

- [ ] All outgoing requests to `https://api.example.com/v2/...` use `Authorization: Bearer <access_token>` — no `X-API-Key` header present.
- [ ] Token exchange correctly POSTs form-encoded `grant_type=client_credentials`, `client_id`, and `client_secret` to `/v2/oauth/token`.
- [ ] Access tokens are cached and reused until near expiry, rather than fetched on every request.
- [ ] Token refresh logic correctly computes expiry from `expires_in` and refreshes before expiry (with a safety buffer).
- [ ] A new token is successfully obtained and used after an old one has expired (test this explicitly, e.g., by forcing a short-lived cache or waiting out an expiry in a test environment).
- [ ] `client_id`/`client_secret` are stored securely (not hardcoded, not logged) and are the v2 credentials, not confused with the v1 API key.
- [ ] v1 `X-API-Key` usage has been fully removed from the codebase once v2 is confirmed working.
- [ ] The v1 API key has been revoked in the dashboard (or you've confirmed a plan to let it lapse no later than the sunset date).
- [ ] No requests depend on v1 auth remaining functional past the sunset date.
